{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py --global-memory-md /workspace/investigation/memory.md --global-memory-jsonl /workspace/investigation/memory.jsonl --event-json '{\\\"step\\\":\\\"paper\\\",\\\"scope\\\":\\\"paper\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"Choose a thesis-first reliability-efficiency audit of frozen scale-adaptive ILR regions; reject causal selector superiority and unimplemented broad geometry/Mondrian stories. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=This frames existing evaluated evidence rather than adding or strengthening a methodological recommendation.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"final.metric.iid_coverage_error=0.013562011718750005; final.metric.shift_coverage_error=0.13034261067708333; final.metric.baseline_utility_gap=0.011594669497844157. Evaluator method records show mean simplex mass 0.3034934467739529 for scaled ILR versus 0.2917009546491835 for simplex L-infinity, despite higher aggregate utility. Condition comparisons show gains concentrated in IID Dirichlet K=8 and losses against simplex controls under shift. Stored paired intervals exist; uncertainty objects repeat across episode wrappers and must be deduplicated. Final selector-specific ablation and original-initializer final results are absent. Shift expands covariate support; its mechanisms are not isolated.\\\",\\\"next_action\\\":\\\"Write mirrored strategy files with three readable figures, substantive 8-9 page plan, supplementary source ledger, final qualification disclosure, and strict search/final separation.\\\"}'; /home/agent-home/.codex/tmp/arg0/codex-arg0bBrrgt/apply_patch <<'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: paper_strategy.json\n+{\n+  \\\"status\\\": \\\"ready\\\",\n+  \\\"selected_prompt_family\\\": \\\"paper_standalone\\\",\n+  \\\"recommended_title\\\": \\\"Scale-Adaptive Compositional Conformal Regions: Reliability and Limits\\\",\n+  \\\"central_question\\\": \\\"Across the tested synthetic response distributions, dimensions, and covariate regimes, how do training-only scale-adaptive ILR conformal regions trade observed-composition coverage against uniform-simplex region mass relative to matched-center alternatives?\\\",\n+  \\\"central_thesis\\\": \\\"In the six tested synthetic settings, scale-adaptive ILR regions exhibit condition-dependent reliability–efficiency trade-offs rather than uniform dominance: near-target IID coverage coexists with shifted Dirichlet undercoverage, and higher aggregate utility does not imply smaller simplex regions or conditional validity.\\\",\n+  \\\"narrative_rationale\\\": \\\"The strongest evidence is the completed final evaluation with eight seed blocks, paired region constructions, three nominal coverage levels, and explicit coverage, reference-mass, and uncertainty records. It supports a bounded empirical study, not a search-success narrative or a causal claim that rank-based selection improves performance. The IID Dirichlet K=8 comparison and the shifted Dirichlet reversals supply complementary evidence for one thesis. Final qualification is independently recorded as completed/qualified/true; this administrative outcome does not certify scientific generality. No downstream_study is present.\\\",\n+  \\\"prior_paper_story_status\\\": \\\"recentered\\\",\n+  \\\"edit_framing_decision\\\": \\\"No edit context, previous_paper manuscript, aggregated bundle, or extra_tasks bundle is staged, so there is no prior manuscript to preserve or rewrite. Recentered denotes narrowing the broad source-task motivation and earlier proposed geometry audit to the actually evaluated fixed-ILR scale-adaptation study. Do not imply that an earlier paper was revised.\\\",\n+  \\\"staged_input_impact\\\": [\n+    \\\"The final evaluator evidence, rather than qualitative memory, sets the main paper scope: logistic-normal IID, Dirichlet IID, and shifted Dirichlet responses, each at K=3 and K=8.\\\",\n+    \\\"The available selected configuration source implements fixed Helmert ILR ridge centers, bounded local-moment scale correction, and nested training-only rank/ILR-box-volume selection; it does not implement the initially proposed geometry comparison or Mondrian calibration.\\\",\n+    \\\"The available Gaussian-selector configuration is useful for describing the intervention but has search-plane evidence only; it is not a final-evaluation selector ablation.\\\",\n+    \\\"Final qualification sources report a completed, qualified evaluation. No failed qualification is present in this run; nevertheless all observed performance limitations remain central.\\\",\n+    \\\"The supplied ICLR style is single-column with 5.5-inch text width and 9-inch text height. This rules out importing a two-column figure plan.\\\",\n+    \\\"Bibliography and provenance files now exist, but retrieval metadata alone does not establish that a work has been read or supports a scientific statement. No additional literature search is needed for strategy generation.\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"method_groups\\\": [\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Training-only scale-adaptive ILR regions\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"proposed\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"The frozen studied configuration: fixed-basis ridge centers, positive diagonal base scales, shrunk scalar local-moment correction, training-only rank-volume selection, and independent pooled split calibration. Proposed denotes the studied configuration, not proven novelty or superiority of its selector.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Unscaled ILR regions with matched centers\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"ablation\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Evaluator-owned unit-scale construction retains the studied method's centers and basis, isolating the effect of the complete scaling package relative to no scaling. It does not isolate the local correction or rank selector separately.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Simplex-coordinate L-infinity regions\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Matched inverse-ILR centers with a jointly calibrated maximum simplex-coordinate residual provide the primary direct comparator; this construction has the largest aggregate utility among the three standard evaluator comparators, but that post-evaluation observation must not be presented as a preregistered selection rule.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Coordinatewise Bonferroni regions\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Matched simplex centers with coordinatewise finite-rank calibration at alpha/K provide a simultaneous-coordinate comparison. Include in the main summary table and full supplemental comparisons rather than creating a separate narrative.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Feature-information and degenerate-region controls\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"supporting\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Constant-center, constant-input, and permuted-input results bound interpretations of feature use. Whole-simplex and zero-radius controls check metric endpoints. These are supporting diagnostics, not rival deployable methods or proof of conditional validity.\\\"\n+    }\n+  ],\n+  \\\"results_focus\\\": [\n+    \\\"Use only final.evidence.episodes for the principal comparisons: eight seeds, six conditions, and nominal coverage 0.95, 0.90, and 0.80. Present the six conditions as a shared design with condition-specific limits, not successive experiments.\\\",\n+    \\\"Lead with observed coverage against nominal coverage, separating IID logistic-normal, IID Dirichlet, and shifted Dirichlet settings. The final mean absolute coverage errors are 0.013562011718750005 for IID and 0.13034261067708333 for shift; these are errors, not signed undercoverage rates.\\\",\n+    \\\"Report a four-method summary table of IID absolute coverage error, shifted absolute coverage error, uniform-simplex mass, and explicitly defined utility. Preserve the equal weighting across seeds, cases, and alpha values; do not pool unequal settings by test-row count.\\\",\n+    \\\"Show paired utility and simplex-mass differences against the simplex-coordinate comparator for every condition and all three alpha values. The selected method has aggregate utility 0.661819668104677 and a comparator gap of 0.011594669497844157, yet its mean simplex mass is 0.3034934467739529 versus approximately 0.291701 for that comparator. This is not a uniform size improvement or an established significant aggregate advantage.\\\",\n+    \\\"Explain the matched-center scaling comparison, especially IID Dirichlet K=8, alongside counterexamples: logistic-normal K=3 does not uniformly benefit from scaling, and shifted Dirichlet comparisons against simplex-coordinate regions reverse the aggregate utility ordering.\\\",\n+    \\\"Distinguish the ILR-box-volume objective used for training-only selection from the uniform-simplex probability mass measured in evaluation. Their different reference measures are factual; a causal account of any observed reversal is not established.\\\",\n+    \\\"Use the available paired seed bootstrap intervals, marginal Wilson intervals, feature-group records, and Monte Carlo resolution diagnostics at their actual levels. Retain unfavorable comparisons and intervals spanning zero; do not select only favorable nominal coverage levels.\\\",\n+    \\\"Do not report campaign winner margins, original-incumbent improvements, or scored/unscored attempt counts: downstream_study and study.* grounding entries are absent. Archived alternatives remain search-only context and cannot be mixed with final performance. The original initializer lacks matched final result records and is omitted as a performance comparator, not silently renamed as the unscaled ablation.\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"figure_plan\\\": [\n+    \\\"Figure 1, early conceptual overview: training-only scale selection, independent calibration, and two distinct evaluation axes; explicitly distinguish the ILR surrogate from simplex reference mass.\\\",\n+    \\\"Figure 2, coverage calibration: two dimension panels with all three response/covariate regimes, all nominal levels, uncertainty, and an identity reference.\\\",\n+    \\\"Figure 3, direct paired trade-offs: four panels showing utility and simplex-mass differences against the simplex-coordinate comparator, with dimensions separated and every nominal level retained.\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"figure_specs\\\": [\n+    {\n+      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"composition_reliability_overview\\\",\n+      \\\"required\\\": true,\n+      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Make the prediction target, training/calibration separation, scale-selection mechanism, and distinct reliability/efficiency estimands legible at a glance.\\\",\n+      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"diagram\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"generate-concept-figure\\\",\n+      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"scientific_stage_not_search_order\\\",\n+      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"observed-composition membership and uniform-simplex reference mass\\\",\n+      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"training-only fitting, independent calibration, evaluation\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n+      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 0.98,\n+      \\\"target_height_inches\\\": 1.6,\n+      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"The ICLR text column is the full 5.5-inch text width. A three-part horizontal overview at about 5.39 inches wide and 1.6 inches high allows short labels at 9–10 pt; do not shrink a crowded diagram. Place on page 1 if naturally possible, otherwise page 2, with about 0.30–0.35 page including caption.\\\",\n+      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n+      \\\"max_panels\\\": 3,\n+      \\\"max_panels_per_row\\\": 3,\n+      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 6,\n+      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 2,\n+      \\\"content\\\": \\\"Training X,Y -> fixed ILR center/base scale -> local-moment correction and nested pseudo-calibration selection; separate calibration Y -> finite-rank threshold -> region for an observed composition; evaluator -> coverage and uniform-simplex probe mass across IID/shift. Put all pseudo-calibration inside the training partition. Use no invented region shapes, numerical measurements, model architectures, or causal arrows from surrogate optimization to empirical gains.\\\",\n+      \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"],\n+      \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"results/ideas/03_training_only_conformal_rank_scale_selection/program_1/src/program.py\\\", \\\"task/evaluate_final.py\\\"],\n+      \\\"outputs\\\": \\\"Preserve editable draw.io source and prompt log. Prefer vector PDF exported from that source when available; otherwise regenerate the overview raster at least 2400 px wide. Never enlarge an existing raster. The later writing/orchestration stage owns generation.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"coverage_by_regime\\\",\n+      \\\"required\\\": true,\n+      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Expose near-target IID behavior and the shifted Dirichlet coverage deficit without hiding nominal-level dependence in an aggregate error.\\\",\n+      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"calibration_curve\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"matplotlib\\\",\n+      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"nominal_coverage\\\",\n+      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"mean observed-composition coverage\\\",\n+      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"K=3 and K=8\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n+      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 0.98,\n+      \\\"target_height_inches\\\": 2.45,\n+      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"Two panels side by side within the single 5.5-inch ICLR column give roughly 2.5-inch plotting widths. Three target values and three regime curves remain readable with shared axes, 9-pt ticks, a common legend, and a thin identity reference; budget about 0.40–0.45 page with caption. Use the observed data range, explicitly mark any truncated coverage axis, and do not force a zero origin that erases the IID differences.\\\",\n+      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n+      \\\"max_panels\\\": 2,\n+      \\\"max_panels_per_row\\\": 2,\n+      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 3,\n+      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 4,\n+      \\\"content\\\": \\\"For the frozen scaled-ILR method only, plot target coverage 0.80, 0.90, 0.95 against empirical coverage for logistic-normal IID, Dirichlet IID, and Dirichlet shift. The fourth series budget is reserved for the identity reference. This is a calibration comparison, not a temporal trajectory. Baseline comparisons appear in Figure 3 and the main table.\\\",\n+      \\\"uncertainty\\\": \\\"The writer may derive deterministic 95% seed-block percentile intervals from the eight existing per-seed coverage records, resampling seeds jointly across levels and cases. Such mean-coverage intervals are not already supplied in the stored uncertainty object and must be labeled as a new analysis of existing results. Do not average per-episode Wilson endpoints or treat test rows as eight-seed replicates.\\\",\n+      \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.metric.iid_coverage_error\\\", \\\"final.metric.shift_coverage_error\\\"],\n+      \\\"outputs\\\": \\\"Save reproducible plot source, vector figures/coverage_by_regime.pdf, and figures/coverage_by_regime.png at 300 DPI; LaTeX must reference the PDF.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"paired_region_tradeoffs\\\",\n+      \\\"required\\\": true,\n+      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Demonstrate condition-dependent utility and region-mass differences, including the shifted counterexamples to uniform dominance.\\\",\n+      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"paired_dot_whisker\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"matplotlib\\\",\n+      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"response_and_covariate_regime\\\",\n+      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"scaled ILR minus simplex-coordinate L-infinity: utility difference and uniform-simplex mass difference\\\",\n+      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"K and metric, arranged as a 2-by-2 grid\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n+      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 0.98,\n+      \\\"target_height_inches\\\": 4.2,\n+      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"Four compact forest panels need the full single-column text width, not a nominal half-column figure. Rows distinguish dimensions; columns distinguish metrics. Three short regime labels and three target markers fit at 9 pt. Use shared scales within each metric column, a common legend, and zero reference lines; budget about 0.60–0.70 page with caption. If labels collide, shorten labels and increase height within this budget rather than reducing type size.\\\",\n+      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n+      \\\"max_panels\\\": 4,\n+      \\\"max_panels_per_row\\\": 2,\n+      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 3,\n+      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 3,\n+      \\\"content\\\": \\\"Within each panel show logistic-normal IID, Dirichlet IID, and Dirichlet shift, with separate markers for alpha=0.05, 0.10, 0.20. Utility difference above zero favors scaled ILR; mass difference below zero indicates smaller regions. These signs have different meanings and must be stated separately, never collapsed into a single success rate. Show all levels rather than a favorable subset.\\\",\n+      \\\"uncertainty\\\": \\\"Use each case's paired_baselines.simplex_linf records: utility_difference with paired_seed_bootstrap95 and volume_difference with paired_volume_bootstrap95. Intervals are descriptive 95% seed-bootstrap intervals. Available bonferroni54_bootstrap_interval values apply to utility comparisons only and belong in the supplemental multiplicity table, not as volume uncertainty or exact familywise certificates.\\\",\n+      \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.metric.baseline_utility_gap\\\"],\n+      \\\"outputs\\\": \\\"Save reproducible plot source, vector figures/paired_region_tradeoffs.pdf, and figures/paired_region_tradeoffs.png at 300 DPI; LaTeX must reference the PDF.\\\"\n+    }\n+  ],\n+  \\\"supplementary_plan\\\": {\n+    \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Provide the reimplementation, complete paired comparisons, subgroup diagnostics, numerical-resolution limits, and audit trail needed to check the narrow main-paper thesis without hiding its shift failures or unfavorable baselines after the references.\\\",\n+    \\\"planned_subsections\\\": [\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Training-only fitting and selection details\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Give the Helmert basis, ridge design and penalties, nested three-fold residual construction, standardized local moments, Gaussian kernel bandwidths, shrinkage and clipping, swapped pseudo-calibration/evaluation halves, exact rank rule, log arithmetic mean ILR-box-volume objective, and Gaussian-selector/tiny-sample fallbacks. Link equations to the available selected source. Static fallback branches are not evidence of measured fallback frequency. Keep the main paper's core score and independence argument in the body.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"result.top_program_01\\\"],\n+        \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"results/ideas/03_training_only_conformal_rank_scale_selection/program_1/src/program.py\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Synthetic data and reproduction specification\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Document the frozen generator, feature map, logistic-normal noise, Dirichlet concentration function and positivity floor, K, independent train/calibration/test substreams, split sizes 1024/512/2048, all eight final seeds, three alpha values, and the uniform-simplex probe scheme. Preserve manifest hashes and documented dependencies. Record reproduction commands and script-to-figure mappings when the writer creates analysis scripts; distinguish documented budgets from measured runtime and do not invent hardware or environment versions.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.qualification.status\\\"],\n+        \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"task/evaluate_final.py\\\", \\\"task/resources/final/manifest.json\\\", \\\"task/requirements.txt\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Complete paired comparison and uncertainty tables\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Include every final case, alpha, and standard comparator with coverage, mass, utility, paired utility differences, paired mass differences, and their existing intervals. Include the supplied multiplicity-adjusted utility bootstrap intervals with their 54-comparison definition and small-seed limitation. The uncertainty object is repeated identically under each final episode: use it once, not as eight independent estimates. Include full matched-center unscaled-ILR and Bonferroni comparisons omitted from the compact main figure.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Feature-group reliability and information controls\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Report coverage for the evaluator's two x0-sign groups, their sample counts, and per-episode Wilson intervals; any across-seed group summary must be derived at seed level. Show constant-center, constant-input, and permuted-input controls, including stored paired feature-control utility differences. Describe the input transformations accurately and do not interpret them as training-time ablations or conditional-coverage guarantees. Treat this as a descriptive diagnostic, not a new subgroup-valid method.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"],\n+        \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"task/evaluate_final.py\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Reference-mass resolution and limiting controls\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Explain 128 sampled contexts and 1024 shared uniform-simplex probes per case, probe granularity, stored zero-hit counts and one-sided bounds, and the mean per-context Monte Carlo diagnostic. Shared probes correlate contexts: do not call that diagnostic a complete standard error for the final mean. A zero-hit estimate is not zero region mass. Include whole-simplex and zero-radius controls, and distinguish per-context resolution from the average across contexts.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"],\n+        \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"task/evaluate_final.py\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Evidence provenance and configuration scope\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Record final completion and qualification separately from performance, source/tree hashes, final versus search data separation, and the absence of a matched final original-initializer or selector-only comparison. Briefly document the joint-center/scale and Gaussian-selector alternatives as archived configurations with search-only evidence, not as ordered milestones or independent final replications. Do not invent study.* entries, campaign accounting, missing losses, search score deltas, or original-incumbent performance. Preserve any later qualification limitation in revision; unknown gate diagnostics stay unknown.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.qualification.status\\\", \\\"final.qualification.outcome\\\", \\\"final.qualification.correct\\\", \\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"result.top_program_02\\\", \\\"result.top_program_03\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"LLM Usage\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Give the precise roles documented in available local generation memories and source artifacts: research ideation, candidate implementation assistance, reference-query planning, and paper-framing assistance; add later writing and figure-generation roles only when they occur. Do not invent model versions, human oversight, independent checking, or undocumented tool use. Keep the disclosure anonymous.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"],\n+        \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"memory.md\\\", \\\"global_memory/memory.md\\\", \\\"results/ideas/03_training_only_conformal_rank_scale_selection/memory.md\\\"]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  },\n+  \\\"forbidden_headings\\\": [\n+    \\\"Search Progression\\\",\n+    \\\"Campaign Phases\\\",\n+    \\\"Milestones\\\",\n+    \\\"First Valid Result\\\",\n+    \\\"Winning Attempt\\\",\n+    \\\"Experiment A / Experiment B\\\",\n+    \\\"From Failure to Success\\\",\n+    \\\"Leaderboard\\\",\n+    \\\"Generalization Guaranteed\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"forbidden_phrases\\\": [\n+    \\\"correctness rate\\\",\n+    \\\"correct and eligible\\\",\n+    \\\"eligible\\\",\n+    \\\"combined_score\\\",\n+    \\\"rubric\\\",\n+    \\\"worker_01\\\",\n+    \\\"worker_02\\\",\n+    \\\"we finally found\\\",\n+    \\\"first successful attempt\\\",\n+    \\\"each iteration improved\\\",\n+    \\\"the search winner proves\\\",\n+    \\\"state-of-the-art performance\\\",\n+    \\\"uniformly smaller prediction regions\\\",\n+    \\\"guaranteed conditional coverage\\\",\n+    \\\"distribution-shift robustness is established\\\",\n+    \\\"zero probe hits prove zero volume\\\",\n+    \\\"rank selection causes the improvement\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"notes_for_writer\\\": [\n+    \\\"Use a thesis-first standalone structure: Introduction; Related Work; Prediction Regions and Evaluation Targets; Training-only Scale Adaptation; Experimental Design and Uncertainty; Reliability–Efficiency Results; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. These are scientific sections, not implementation or discovery chronology.\\\",\n+    \\\"Budget approximately 8.6 body pages, excluding references: Introduction/abstract/overview 1.2; Related Work 0.55; regions and estimands 0.9; method 1.35; design and uncertainty 1.05; results including Figures 2–3 and the summary table 2.35; discussion/limitations 0.95; conclusion 0.25. Figure space is already included. Reflow within 8–9 pages without changing template geometry; an eight-page body requires its last page at least 75% full. Spend spare space on equations, paired comparisons, uncertainty interpretation, and limitations, not extra chronology or decorative figures.\\\",\n+    \\\"The inspected supplied ICLR article template and paper.sty define one 5.5-inch text column and 9-inch text height. Full_width means that same text column, not a two-column float. A half-width inset would be about 2.64 inches at fraction 0.48 and is unsuitable for the required multi-panel figures. Keep 9-pt or larger final-size plot labels and captions consistent with venue style.\\\",\n+    \\\"Follow VENUE.md: anonymous ICLR 2026 submission, no names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, identifying links, or authorship-revealing first-person prior-work references. The supplied template contains a populated author placeholder and final-copy command; do not carry those into an anonymized submission unchanged. Preserve required infrastructure watermark behavior without introducing identifying authorship.\\\",\n+    \\\"Contributions are a fully specified training-only scale-adaptive configuration, a paired evaluation separating coverage and reference mass, and bounded empirical findings and failure diagnostics. Do not claim a new conformal theorem, comprehensive benchmark, unique new geometry, or causal selector advantage. The existing comparisons do not isolate rank selection from the Gaussian selector in the final plane.\\\",\n+    \\\"State the score and region explicitly: z=log(y)B, r=max_j |z_j-mu_j(x)|/s_j(x), q from order ceil((n_cal+1)(1-alpha)), and C(x)={positive simplex y:r<=q}. If the rank exceeds n_cal, the calibration threshold is infinite. Training pseudo-calibration has its own documented fallback. The standard split-conformal exchangeability argument is background, conditional on the fitted training-only rule; it is neither a measured guarantee under shift nor a conditional-on-X guarantee.\\\",\n+    \\\"Describe the correction as a covariate-dependent scalar multiplier shared across ILR coordinates, applied to positive diagonal base scales. The center and base-scale fits are fixed across the local-correction selectors; the evaluator's unscaled ablation instead sets all scales to one. Do not equate these two different controls.\\\",\n+    \\\"Define reference mass as probability under Dirichlet(1,...,1), averaged over sampled test contexts; it is normalized simplex volume, not ILR-box volume or an absolute Euclidean-volume number. Raw masses at K=3 and K=8 are not directly comparable as intrinsic efficiency rankings. Do not claim exact structural-zero support: responses are positive and the Dirichlet generator applies a declared positivity floor followed by closure.\\\",\n+    \\\"Define utility U=(1-V) max(0,1-|coverage-(1-alpha)|/(1-alpha)). Its absolute-error penalty includes overcoverage, and a higher U is not itself a coverage-validity certificate. Treat U as a benchmark-specific trade-off summary alongside the separate components, not as the paper's sole result or an externally standardized metric.\\\",\n+    \\\"Keep key quantitative anchors in the body, including the shifted failures and the selected method's larger aggregate mass than simplex-coordinate L-infinity. All new derived baseline aggregates, confidence intervals, rounding, and tables must be regenerated from the final records by saved analysis code; they are not additional experiments. Do not claim a significant overall utility advantage from the selected method's own score interval.\\\",\n+    \\\"Final status and qualification must be grounded only in final.qualification.status, final.qualification.outcome, and final.qualification.correct: this run reports completed, qualified, and true. Preserve that outcome without converting it to generalization, conditional validity, or superiority. If later authoritative evidence reports not_qualified, disclose it in writing, review, audit, and revision without attributing the cause unless gate evidence identifies it; missing diagnostics stay unknown.\\\",\n+    \\\"Use evaluator-owned method statistics, not unverified candidate diagnostics, as results. The evidence wrapper labels candidate_diagnostics_trust as unverified. Preserve final source/result hashes in the audit trail. The archived selected source is linked through the available configuration records and final evidence; do not claim an independently rehashed local final source tree if it has not been recovered and checked.\\\",\n+    \\\"Private-only grounding context: available identifiers use result.* and final.*, not study.*. Name the frozen evaluated method by its scientific mechanism. Omit campaign winner assertions, search-score margins, and scored/unscored accounting without the required study.* sources; never count missing measurements as losses. Search and final seed sets differ and must not be pooled into a larger replication count or compared as performance improvement over time.\\\",\n+    \\\"Use eight seeds as the outer repeated unit, not six cases times three alpha levels times eight seeds as independent experiments. Each seed wrapper repeats the same seed_block_uncertainty object; read one copy. For any derived aggregate comparison, resample complete seed blocks jointly across cases, alpha values, and methods. Stored Wilson intervals describe binomial test uncertainty within a fitted episode, not variability over retraining or simultaneous subgroup validity.\\\",\n+    \\\"Interpret existing paired bootstrap intervals descriptively with only eight seeds. The supplied 54-comparison adjusted utility endpoints are bootstrap percentile endpoints at adjusted tails, not a finite-sample familywise coverage theorem. Do not infer exact significance from them; no adjusted volume interval is supplied.\\\",\n+    \\\"The shift stress changes test covariates from training uniform support [-1.5,1.5] to clipped normal covariates on [-2.5,3]. It therefore includes support expansion and loss of exchangeability; it does not isolate which mechanism causes undercoverage. The conditional generator is retained, but support mismatch prevents promising that ordinary density-ratio weighting would repair the failures. No weighted-conformal comparator was evaluated.\\\",\n+    \\\"Keep the main limitations explicit: synthetic responses only, K=3 and K=8 only, fixed ILR basis and region family, pooled rather than subgroup calibration, no exact structural zeros, limited seeds and nominal levels, finite probe resolution, surrogate/reference-measure mismatch, absent final selector-only ablation, and no matched final original-initializer comparison. No CIFAR, classification-label coverage, latent-probability recovery, Hellinger comparison, Mondrian method, or real-data transfer result is supported.\\\",\n+    \\\"Use condition colors consistently across overview and quantitative panels; encode alpha by marker shape and method by labels or line style rather than reusing a condition color for a different meaning. Do not add trajectory plots: the nominal-level calibration axis is not a search iteration axis.\\\",\n+    \\\"Quantitative figure source must export both PDF and 300-DPI PNG. Reference PDFs from LaTeX whenever available. Raster-only full-width assets require at least 2400 pixels; any future column-width raster inset requires at least 1200 pixels. Regenerate from source rather than upscaling. Plan exactly one generate-concept-figure overview; do not run its rendering pipeline in this strategy stage.\\\",\n+    \\\"After references, use clearpage, appendix, and a Supplementary Information section with the grounded subsections above, including a separate LLM Usage section. Keep thesis-essential shift and unfavorable comparisons in the body. Later authoritative appendix rankings are additional routing instructions, not a reason to retroactively invent or rewrite this plan.\\\",\n+    \\\"Bibliography/provenance exist but contain many off-topic retrievals and bibliographic ambiguities. Use only relevant, actually consulted and verified sources; metadata is not a read-paper claim. Lei2016DistributionFreePI and Yang2021SelectionAA are potentially relevant existing keys, not automatically endorsed citations. Verify publication versions/years and scientific statements before use. Do not search for, identify, infer, access, or follow citation links to the prohibited study; stop and log any accidental encounter. Do not inflate related work with unrelated application citations.\\\",\n+    \\\"This stage creates strategy and memory artifacts only. Do not draft paper.tex or claims_grounding.json, generate figures, run new research experiments, or invoke final-paper validation here. Check strategy JSON syntax, required fields, source-ID existence, readability budgets, and memo agreement instead.\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"failure_reason\\\": \\\"\\\"\n+}\n*** Add File: paper_strategy.md\n+# Paper Strategy\n+\n+## Decision\n+\n+**Status:** ready for \"'`paper_standalone`, using `write-paper-standalone` for the later manuscript stage. This is a plan, not a paper draft.\n+\n+**Provisional title:** Scale-Adaptive Compositional Conformal Regions: Reliability and Limits\n+\n+**Central question:** Across the tested synthetic response distributions, dimensions, and covariate regimes, how do training-only scale-adaptive ILR conformal regions trade observed-composition coverage against uniform-simplex region mass relative to matched-center alternatives?\n+\n+**Central thesis:** In the six tested synthetic settings, scale-adaptive ILR regions exhibit condition-dependent reliability–efficiency trade-offs rather than uniform dominance: near-target IID coverage coexists with shifted Dirichlet undercoverage, and higher aggregate utility does not imply smaller simplex regions or conditional validity.\n+\n+The paper is one controlled empirical study of a fixed region family. Logistic-normal IID, Dirichlet IID, and shifted Dirichlet conditions, each at K=3 and K=8, are evidence blocks under the same question—not sequential experiments or a claim of transfer to arbitrary domains. The scientific value is a reproducible method specification and a reliability–efficiency assessment with concrete failures, not a claim that a small aggregate gain establishes a new universally superior method.\n+\n+## Framing Decision and Alternatives\n+\n+No edit context, seeded prior manuscript, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` bundle is present. The JSON decision `recentered` means narrowing the original broad task and proposed geometry audit to actual evaluated evidence; it does not assert that a prior paper was rewritten.\n+\n+| Candidate framing | Decision and grounded reason |\n+| --- | --- |\n+| Preserve an earlier broad geometry/boundary audit with local updates | Reject. No prior manuscript is available, and Hellinger/Euclidean geometry comparisons, structural-zero face audits, and Mondrian calibration were proposed rather than demonstrated. |\n+| Broaden into a cross-setting conformal method family | Retain only within the six synthetic conditions sharing the fixed ILR construction. No real-data, architecture, classification-label, or alternative-geometry transfer evidence supports a larger family claim. |\n+| Recenter on rank-selector superiority | Reject as the central thesis. Source code supports the mechanism, but the final evidence lacks a matched Gaussian-versus-rank selector ablation. Search-plane configurations do not establish a causal final-plane gain. |\n+| Recenter on reliability–efficiency trade-offs of the frozen scale-adaptive regions | Select. Final paired comparisons, independent calibration, multiple nominal levels, and uncertainty records support both favorable IID behavior and adverse shifted results. |\n+\n+Inspecting the selected source materially narrows the story: fixed Helmert ILR ridge centers, positive diagonal base scales, a bounded covariate-dependent scalar local-moment correction, and nested training-only rank/ILR-box-volume selection. The scalar correction is common to the ILR coordinates for a given covariate vector; it is not a separately learned full covariance geometry.\n+\n+## Final Evidence and Qualification\n+\n+Read `paper_data.json.final_evaluation` independently of any campaign summary. `downstream_study` and `study.*` grounding entries are absent. The final qualification sources explicitly report:\n+\n+- `final.qualification.status`: `completed`.\n+- `final.qualification.outcome`: `qualified`.\n+- `final.qualification.correct`: `true`.\n+\n+There is no failed qualification to disclose in this run. Qualification nevertheless does not establish superiority, conditional coverage, or generalization. If authoritative later artifacts report failed qualification, preserve that outcome and its limitations in writing, review, audit, and revision. Attribute a failure to statistical performance, validity, or adaptivity only when evaluator diagnostics identify the cause; missing gate diagnostics remain unknown.\n+\n+`final.evidence.episodes` contains eight final seed blocks, with six completed cases per seed and three alpha values per case. Final seeds are 1009, 1013, 1019, 1021, 1031, 1033, 1039, and 1049. Nominal coverage levels are 0.95, 0.90, and 0.80. This is not a set of independent case-by-alpha replications: seeds are the outer repeated unit. The identical uncertainty summary repeated under each seed wrapper must be read once.\n+\n+Important grounded anchors:\n+\n+| Quantity | Value and grounding |\n+| --- | --- |\n+| Studied method'\"'s mean IID absolute coverage error | 0.013562011718750005; \"'`final.metric.iid_coverage_error` |\n+| Studied method'\"'s mean shifted absolute coverage error | 0.13034261067708333; \"'`final.metric.shift_coverage_error` |\n+| Studied method'\"'s mean uniform-simplex mass | 0.3034934467739529; \"'`final.metric.mean_volume` |\n+| Studied method'\"'s mean utility | 0.661819668104677; \"'`final.metric.final_score` |\n+| Gap to the largest aggregate utility among standard evaluator comparators | 0.011594669497844157; `final.metric.baseline_utility_gap` |\n+| Simplex-coordinate L-infinity mean mass | Approximately 0.291701, derived from evaluator method records under `final.evidence.episodes` |\n+\n+These are retained measurements, not certification of a significant aggregate advantage. In particular, larger mean mass than the simplex-coordinate comparator contradicts a blanket size-improvement claim. Absolute coverage error is not signed undercoverage; signed coverage must be shown separately. The existing overall score interval cannot establish a confidence interval for the difference between methods.\n+\n+The wrapper labels candidate diagnostics as unverified. Use evaluator-owned membership and method statistics, not candidate assertions. Preserve evaluator/program/result hashes in the audit trail. The available selected source is linked by configuration records; do not assert that a locally recovered final source tree has been independently rehashed when it has not.\n+\n+## Method Groups and Comparison Roles\n+\n+1. **Training-only scale-adaptive ILR regions — studied/proposed configuration.** Explain the center, base scale, correction family, training-only selector, and independent pooled calibration. “Proposed” is an organizational role, not a claim of unique novelty.\n+2. **Unscaled ILR — matched-center ablation.** Set scales to one, retaining the same center and ILR basis. This compares the whole scaling package against no scaling; it does not isolate the correction or selector.\n+3. **Simplex-coordinate L-infinity — primary direct baseline.** Use the inverse-ILR center and a joint maximum-coordinate residual. Include all comparisons, not only conditions favoring the studied method. Its largest aggregate utility among standard comparators is an observed result, not a preregistered comparator-selection rule.\n+4. **Coordinatewise Bonferroni — simultaneous-coordinate baseline.** Use per-coordinate ranks at alpha/K. Include in the main summary table and complete supplemental comparisons.\n+5. **Feature-information and degenerate-region controls — supporting diagnostics.** Constant-center, constant-input, permuted-input, whole-simplex, and zero-radius records assess feature information and metric endpoints. They are not additional deployable method families or conditional-validity evidence.\n+\n+The original initializer source exists, but matched final performance records for it do not. Omit that performance comparison; do not relabel the unscaled ablation as the original initializer. Describe the archived joint-center/scale and Gaussian-selector configurations only in supplementary provenance as search-only alternatives, not final ablations. Without `study.*`, omit campaign winner claims, search-score margins, and scored/unscored accounting. Missing measurements must never become measured losses.\n+\n+## Scientific Organization and Results Views\n+\n+Use these body sections:\n+\n+1. Introduction.\n+2. Related Work.\n+3. Prediction Regions and Evaluation Targets.\n+4. Training-only Scale Adaptation.\n+5. Experimental Design and Uncertainty.\n+6. Reliability–Efficiency Results.\n+7. Discussion and Limitations.\n+8. Conclusion.\n+\n+The contribution list should be limited to a reimplementable training-only configuration, a matched-center evaluation separating reliability from region mass, and empirical condition-dependent findings with failure diagnostics. No new conformal theorem, comprehensive benchmark, or causal selector improvement is established.\n+\n+The main results should answer three scientific questions:\n+\n+- **Does measured coverage track the target?** Show all nominal levels, with IID and shifted conditions separate. Shift failure belongs in the body, not only the supplement.\n+- **When does scaling change the trade-off?** The IID Dirichlet K=8 comparison is informative, but retain the logistic-normal K=3 counterexample and shifted Dirichlet utility reversals. No uniform dominance claim survives those comparisons.\n+- **Does a higher utility mean smaller or more reliable regions?** No. Show separate coverage and mass alongside utility. Explain that the training selector minimizes an ILR-box surrogate, whereas evaluation uses uniform-simplex mass. Different measures are factual; their causal contribution to the observed results is unresolved.\n+\n+Include one compact main table with four method rows and columns for IID absolute coverage error, shifted absolute coverage error, mean reference mass, and utility. Recompute baseline aggregates from the final records using saved code. Equal weighting is across seeds, cases, and nominal levels, not a pooled mixture weighted by subgroup sample counts. Define utility rather than using an internal score label:\n+\n+`U = (1 - V) max(0, 1 - |coverage - (1-alpha)| / (1-alpha))`.\n+\n+Its error penalty includes overcoverage; it is a benchmark-specific summary, not a validity test or a standardized universal objective. Do not use a thresholded success-rate figure.\n+\n+## Required Figures and Layout\n+\n+The supplied ICLR template uses `article` and `paper.sty`. The inspected style sets one **5.5-inch text column** and **9-inch text height**. Here full-width and column-width refer to the same physical text width; there is no two-column `figure*` assumption. A 0.48-width inset would be approximately 2.64 inches and is unsuitable for these dense comparisons. The required figures use about 5.39 inches at fraction 0.98. Keep final-size labels at least 9 pt and shorten labels instead of shrinking a crowded figure.\n+\n+### Figure 1 — `composition_reliability_overview`\n+\n+- **Purpose:** immediate understanding of observed-composition prediction, training/calibration separation, and coverage versus reference mass.\n+- **Generator:** `generate-concept-figure`; exactly one overview uses this workflow. Do not render it in this stage.\n+- **Footprint:** full text width, approximately 5.39 by 1.6 inches; about 0.30–0.35 page including caption. Place on page 1 if natural, otherwise page 2.\n+- **Readability budget:** at most three panels in one row, six short nodes/categories per panel, and two information streams per panel; labels 9–10 pt.\n+- **Content:** training-only center/scale fitting and local-moment/rank-volume selection; separate finite-rank calibration; observed-composition membership and uniform-simplex probe evaluation across IID/shift. Pseudo-calibration stays inside the training partition. Label the ILR surrogate and simplex evaluation measure distinctly.\n+- **Grounding:** `final.evidence.episodes`; selected source at `results/ideas/03_training_only_conformal_rank_scale_selection/program_1/src/program.py`; evaluator definitions in `task/evaluate_final.py`.\n+- **Cautions:** no invented region measurements, alternative geometries, architectural blocks, or arrows implying a proven causal gain. This is a scientific data-partition schematic, not a research-run timeline.\n+\n+### Figure 2 — `coverage_by_regime`\n+\n+- **Purpose:** show near-target IID coverage and shifted Dirichlet deficits across every target level.\n+- **Generator:** matplotlib calibration curves, not a search progression plot.\n+- **Footprint:** full text width, approximately 5.39 by 2.45 inches; about 0.40–0.45 page with caption. Two panels side by side for K=3 and K=8.\n+- **Readability budget:** two panels; three target positions; three regime curves plus the identity reference, at most four series total per panel. Share axes and legend.\n+- **Content:** studied method only, target coverage 0.80/0.90/0.95 versus empirical coverage; logistic-normal IID, Dirichlet IID, and Dirichlet shift. Baseline evidence remains in Figure 3 and the main table. Use the observed coverage range and explicitly mark a truncated axis rather than flattening IID differences with an unhelpful zero origin.\n+- **Uncertainty:** the writer may derive deterministic 95% seed-block percentile intervals from the eight existing coverage records, resampling seeds jointly across cases and targets. These intervals are a later analysis of retained measurements, not an already available stored mean-coverage interval. Do not average per-episode Wilson endpoints.\n+- **Grounding:** `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.iid_coverage_error`, and `final.metric.shift_coverage_error`.\n+\n+### Figure 3 — `paired_region_tradeoffs`\n+\n+- **Purpose:** directly show condition-dependent utility and region-mass differences against simplex-coordinate L-infinity, including adverse shifted comparisons.\n+- **Generator:** matplotlib paired dot-and-whisker comparisons.\n+- **Footprint:** full text width, approximately 5.39 by 4.2 inches; about 0.60–0.70 page including caption. Four panels in a 2-by-2 grid; rows are K and columns are utility difference versus mass difference.\n+- **Readability budget:** four panels, two per row; three regime categories and three alpha markers per panel. Shared scales within each metric column; a common legend and zero-reference lines.\n+- **Content:** scaled ILR minus comparator, for every regime at alpha 0.05/0.10/0.20. Utility above zero favors scaled ILR; mass below zero indicates smaller regions. State both sign meanings explicitly rather than inventing one common success threshold.\n+- **Uncertainty:** use `utility_difference`/`paired_seed_bootstrap95` and `volume_difference`/`paired_volume_bootstrap95` under each case'\"'s \"'`paired_baselines.simplex_linf`. Descriptive 95% intervals are not simultaneous certificates. The stored adjusted utility endpoints are supplemental context and must not be applied to mass differences.\n+- **Grounding:** `final.evidence.episodes` and `final.metric.baseline_utility_gap`.\n+\n+Use consistent condition colors across the overview and plots; alpha can use marker shape and methods can use labels/line style. Never reuse a condition hue to mean a different method or threshold. Export quantitative figures from reproducible source as **PDF plus PNG at 300 DPI**, and reference the PDF from LaTeX. Preserve overview draw.io source and prompt logs; use a vector PDF export if available. Raster-only full-width figures require at least **2400 px** width, and any future column-width raster inset requires at least **1200 px**. Regenerate from source; never upscale an existing raster.\n+\n+## Body Budget and Venue Fit\n+\n+Target about **8.6 body pages**, excluding references, within `VENUE.md`'\"'s 8–9 page requirement:\n+\n+| Body material, including its figures/tables | Approximate pages |\n+| --- | ---: |\n+| Abstract, introduction, and early overview | 1.20 |\n+| Related work | 0.55 |\n+| Prediction regions and estimands | 0.90 |\n+| Training-only scale-adaptation method | 1.35 |\n+| Experimental design and uncertainty | 1.05 |\n+| Results, Figures 2–3, and summary table | 2.35 |\n+| Discussion and limitations | 0.95 |\n+| Conclusion | 0.25 |\n+\n+These allocations include figure space; do not add it again. If the body ends at eight pages, the final page must be at least 75% full. Reflow within nine pages without changing margins or fonts. Spend additional room on the split-independence argument, precise objectives, complete paired comparisons, uncertainty levels, and scope analysis—not filler, redundant plots, or chronological search details.\n+\n+Follow the anonymous ICLR 2026 brief: omit names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, and identifying links. The supplied template has a populated author placeholder and final-copy setting; do not preserve them unchanged in an anonymized submission. Preserve required infrastructure watermark behavior. Include a separate \"'`LLM Usage` section after the references.\n+\n+## Method and Statistical Guardrails\n+\n+- Define `z=log(y)B`, the maximum standardized ILR residual, independent split-calibration order `ceil((n_cal+1)(1-alpha))`, and the region membership rule. The evaluator uses an infinite threshold if the rank exceeds calibration size; the training pseudo-calibration selector has a distinct Gaussian fallback. State the usual exchangeability argument as background, not a new theorem or a conditional-on-X guarantee.\n+- Explain the actual scalar local correction, held-out selection, and fixed base fits. The unscaled ablation is not the unchanged-base-scale control, and no final local-correction-only or selector-only attribution is available.\n+- Measure `V` under Dirichlet(1,...,1), averaged over sampled contexts. It is normalized simplex volume, not ILR-box volume or an unnormalized Euclidean-volume measurement. Do not rank intrinsic efficiency by comparing raw K=3 and K=8 masses.\n+- Use eight seed blocks for variability over fitting and evaluation. Resample complete seed blocks jointly for any new aggregate contrast; do not pretend the repeated case/alpha cells are independent seeds. Saved Wilson intervals concern test uncertainty within a fitted episode and are not retraining or simultaneous subgroup intervals.\n+- Existing paired percentile intervals are descriptive with only eight seeds. The stored adjusted utility intervals use 54 comparisons and adjusted bootstrap tails; they are not an exact finite-sample familywise theorem. No corresponding adjusted mass interval is supplied.\n+- The reference-mass estimator uses 128 contexts and 1024 shared probes per case. Shared probes induce dependence across contexts. The stored mean per-context Monte Carlo diagnostic is not a complete standard error of the grand mean. Zero hits do not prove zero mass, and per-context probe resolution differs from averaged resolution.\n+- The shift recipe changes test X from uniform training support [-1.5,1.5] to a clipped normal on [-2.5,3], retaining the conditional generator. This includes support expansion as well as nonexchangeability. Undercoverage is measured; extrapolation, weighting, and model mechanisms are not separately identified. Do not promise that ordinary importance weighting would repair the result, and do not imply weighted conformal was evaluated.\n+- Keep limitations in the main paper: synthetic data only; fixed basis/region family; K=3 or 8 only; pooled calibration; no structural-zero evaluation; limited seeds/levels; Monte Carlo resolution; different selection/evaluation measures; missing final selector-only and original-initializer comparisons. Do not add CIFAR, label-set, latent-probability, Hellinger, Mondrian, or real-data claims.\n+\n+## Supplementary Information\n+\n+After references, use `clearpage`, appendix, and a **Supplementary Information** section. This adds substantive depth, not a dumping ground for adverse thesis-essential findings. Planned grounded subsections mirror the JSON:\n+\n+1. **Training-only fitting and selection details.** Basis, ridge design/penalties, three-fold residual construction, standardized moments, smoothing bandwidths, shrinkage/clipping, swapped pseudo-calibration halves, finite ranks, log arithmetic mean ILR-box objective, and static fallbacks. Ground in `final.evidence.episodes`, `result.top_program_01`, and the selected source. Do not invent realized fallback frequencies.\n+2. **Synthetic data and reproduction specification.** Generator equations, positivity floor, substreams, K, split sizes 1024/512/2048, final seeds/alphas, manifests, hashes, probe design, and documented NumPy dependency. Use `task/evaluate_final.py`, `task/resources/final/manifest.json`, and `task/requirements.txt`; grounding anchors `final.evidence.episodes` and `final.qualification.status`. The writer must save actual analysis commands and figure-script mappings, not invent runtime, hardware, or environment versions.\n+3. **Complete paired comparison and uncertainty tables.** Every case/alpha/standard baseline, full utility and mass contrasts, descriptive and adjusted utility endpoints, and the deduplicated uncertainty record. Ground in `final.evidence.episodes`. Keep essential shifted failures and unfavorable comparisons in the main body as well.\n+4. **Feature-group reliability and information controls.** Evaluator x0-sign groups, sample counts, Wilson intervals, and feature-control contrasts. Any mean group coverage interval must be computed at seed level. Ground in `final.evidence.episodes` and the evaluator. These are descriptive audits, not Mondrian validity or training-time feature ablations.\n+5. **Reference-mass resolution and limiting controls.** Shared probes, zero-hit bounds, per-context granularity, Monte Carlo diagnostic limitations, whole-simplex/zero-radius endpoints. Ground in `final.evidence.episodes` and evaluator definitions.\n+6. **Evidence provenance and configuration scope.** Separate qualification from performance and search from final evidence; retain source/result hashes and describe archived joint-center/scale and Gaussian-selector configurations without an ordered success story. Ground in `final.qualification.*`, `final.evidence.episodes`, `result.top_program_02`, and `result.top_program_03`. Do not report unsupported original-incumbent or campaign-count comparisons.\n+7. **LLM Usage.** Disclose documented ideation, implementation assistance, reference-query planning, and framing roles from local/global memories and source artifacts. Add later writing/figure-generation roles only when performed. `final.evidence.episodes` anchors the study; process disclosure is independently supported by `memory.md`, `global_memory/memory.md`, and the selected configuration'\"'s memory. Do not invent model versions, human oversight, or independent checks, and keep the disclosure anonymous.\n+\n+Later authoritative appendix-ranked analyses should join this supplement without retroactively inventing rankings in the strategy.\n+\n+## Citation and Language Discipline\n+\n+\"'`references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` now exist, but many retrieved entries are off-topic or have ambiguous publication versions. Metadata retrieval does not mean a work was read. `Lei2016DistributionFreePI` and `Yang2021SelectionAA` are potentially relevant existing keys, not automatically approved scientific support. Before citation, verify the relevant source, publication identity/year, and the claimed statement. Do not pad related work with unrelated applications or invent missing references.\n+\n+Do not identify, infer, search for, access, or follow citation networks to the prohibited study. No external literature browsing is part of this strategy stage. If later work accidentally encounters prohibited material, stop reading and record the location and amount seen.\n+\n+Forbidden headings include **Search Progression**, **Campaign Phases**, **Milestones**, **First Valid Result**, **Winning Attempt**, **Experiment A / Experiment B**, **From Failure to Success**, **Leaderboard**, and **Generalization Guaranteed**.\n+\n+Forbidden visible phrases include pipeline labels such as `correctness rate`, `correct and eligible`, `eligible`, `combined_score`, `rubric`, `worker_01`, and `worker_02`; chronology language such as “we finally found,” “first successful attempt,” and “each iteration improved”; and unsupported claims such as “state-of-the-art performance,” “uniformly smaller prediction regions,” “guaranteed conditional coverage,” “distribution-shift robustness is established,” “zero probe hits prove zero volume,” or “rank selection causes the improvement.” Internal source keys belong only in private grounding/audit context, not reader-facing result labels.\n+\n+## Handoff and Checks\n+\n+Implement the required figures before optional additions. Derive new summaries only from retained final records with saved analysis code; no new experiment is required for this strategy. This stage writes neither `paper.tex` nor `claims_grounding.json`, generates no figures, and does not run final-paper validation. Its checks are JSON syntax, required fields, source-ID existence, figure readability budgets, and agreement between this memo and the JSON.\n*** End Patch\nPATCH'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py --global-memory-md /workspace/investigation/memory.md --global-memory-jsonl /workspace/investigation/memory.jsonl --event-json '{\\\"step\\\":\\\"paper\\\",\\\"scope\\\":\\\"paper\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"Choose a thesis-first reliability-efficiency audit of frozen scale-adaptive ILR regions; reject causal selector superiority and unimplemented broad geometry/Mondrian stories. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=This frames existing evaluated evidence rather than adding or strengthening a methodological recommendation.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"final.metric.iid_coverage_error=0.013562011718750005; final.metric.shift_coverage_error=0.13034261067708333; final.metric.baseline_utility_gap=0.011594669497844157. Evaluator method records show mean simplex mass 0.3034934467739529 for scaled ILR versus 0.2917009546491835 for simplex L-infinity, despite higher aggregate utility. Condition comparisons show gains concentrated in IID Dirichlet K=8 and losses against simplex controls under shift. Stored paired intervals exist; uncertainty objects repeat across episode wrappers and must be deduplicated. Final selector-specific ablation and original-initializer final results are absent. Shift expands covariate support; its mechanisms are not isolated.\\\",\\\"next_action\\\":\\\"Write mirrored strategy files with three readable figures, substantive 8-9 page plan, supplementary source ledger, final qualification disclosure, and strict search/final separation.\\\"}'; /home/agent-home/.codex/tmp/arg0/codex-arg0bBrrgt/apply_patch <<'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: paper_strategy.json\n+{\n+  \\\"status\\\": \\\"ready\\\",\n+  \\\"selected_prompt_family\\\": \\\"paper_standalone\\\",\n+  \\\"recommended_title\\\": \\\"Scale-Adaptive Compositional Conformal Regions: Reliability and Limits\\\",\n+  \\\"central_question\\\": \\\"Across the tested synthetic response distributions, dimensions, and covariate regimes, how do training-only scale-adaptive ILR conformal regions trade observed-composition coverage against uniform-simplex region mass relative to matched-center alternatives?\\\",\n+  \\\"central_thesis\\\": \\\"In the six tested synthetic settings, scale-adaptive ILR regions exhibit condition-dependent reliability–efficiency trade-offs rather than uniform dominance: near-target IID coverage coexists with shifted Dirichlet undercoverage, and higher aggregate utility does not imply smaller simplex regions or conditional validity.\\\",\n+  \\\"narrative_rationale\\\": \\\"The strongest evidence is the completed final evaluation with eight seed blocks, paired region constructions, three nominal coverage levels, and explicit coverage, reference-mass, and uncertainty records. It supports a bounded empirical study, not a search-success narrative or a causal claim that rank-based selection improves performance. The IID Dirichlet K=8 comparison and the shifted Dirichlet reversals supply complementary evidence for one thesis. Final qualification is independently recorded as completed/qualified/true; this administrative outcome does not certify scientific generality. No downstream_study is present.\\\",\n+  \\\"prior_paper_story_status\\\": \\\"recentered\\\",\n+  \\\"edit_framing_decision\\\": \\\"No edit context, previous_paper manuscript, aggregated bundle, or extra_tasks bundle is staged, so there is no prior manuscript to preserve or rewrite. Recentered denotes narrowing the broad source-task motivation and earlier proposed geometry audit to the actually evaluated fixed-ILR scale-adaptation study. Do not imply that an earlier paper was revised.\\\",\n+  \\\"staged_input_impact\\\": [\n+    \\\"The final evaluator evidence, rather than qualitative memory, sets the main paper scope: logistic-normal IID, Dirichlet IID, and shifted Dirichlet responses, each at K=3 and K=8.\\\",\n+    \\\"The available selected configuration source implements fixed Helmert ILR ridge centers, bounded local-moment scale correction, and nested training-only rank/ILR-box-volume selection; it does not implement the initially proposed geometry comparison or Mondrian calibration.\\\",\n+    \\\"The available Gaussian-selector configuration is useful for describing the intervention but has search-plane evidence only; it is not a final-evaluation selector ablation.\\\",\n+    \\\"Final qualification sources report a completed, qualified evaluation. No failed qualification is present in this run; nevertheless all observed performance limitations remain central.\\\",\n+    \\\"The supplied ICLR style is single-column with 5.5-inch text width and 9-inch text height. This rules out importing a two-column figure plan.\\\",\n+    \\\"Bibliography and provenance files now exist, but retrieval metadata alone does not establish that a work has been read or supports a scientific statement. No additional literature search is needed for strategy generation.\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"method_groups\\\": [\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Training-only scale-adaptive ILR regions\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"proposed\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"The frozen studied configuration: fixed-basis ridge centers, positive diagonal base scales, shrunk scalar local-moment correction, training-only rank-volume selection, and independent pooled split calibration. Proposed denotes the studied configuration, not proven novelty or superiority of its selector.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Unscaled ILR regions with matched centers\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"ablation\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Evaluator-owned unit-scale construction retains the studied method's centers and basis, isolating the effect of the complete scaling package relative to no scaling. It does not isolate the local correction or rank selector separately.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Simplex-coordinate L-infinity regions\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Matched inverse-ILR centers with a jointly calibrated maximum simplex-coordinate residual provide the primary direct comparator; this construction has the largest aggregate utility among the three standard evaluator comparators, but that post-evaluation observation must not be presented as a preregistered selection rule.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Coordinatewise Bonferroni regions\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Matched simplex centers with coordinatewise finite-rank calibration at alpha/K provide a simultaneous-coordinate comparison. Include in the main summary table and full supplemental comparisons rather than creating a separate narrative.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Feature-information and degenerate-region controls\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"supporting\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"Constant-center, constant-input, and permuted-input results bound interpretations of feature use. Whole-simplex and zero-radius controls check metric endpoints. These are supporting diagnostics, not rival deployable methods or proof of conditional validity.\\\"\n+    }\n+  ],\n+  \\\"results_focus\\\": [\n+    \\\"Use only final.evidence.episodes for the principal comparisons: eight seeds, six conditions, and nominal coverage 0.95, 0.90, and 0.80. Present the six conditions as a shared design with condition-specific limits, not successive experiments.\\\",\n+    \\\"Lead with observed coverage against nominal coverage, separating IID logistic-normal, IID Dirichlet, and shifted Dirichlet settings. The final mean absolute coverage errors are 0.013562011718750005 for IID and 0.13034261067708333 for shift; these are errors, not signed undercoverage rates.\\\",\n+    \\\"Report a four-method summary table of IID absolute coverage error, shifted absolute coverage error, uniform-simplex mass, and explicitly defined utility. Preserve the equal weighting across seeds, cases, and alpha values; do not pool unequal settings by test-row count.\\\",\n+    \\\"Show paired utility and simplex-mass differences against the simplex-coordinate comparator for every condition and all three alpha values. The selected method has aggregate utility 0.661819668104677 and a comparator gap of 0.011594669497844157, yet its mean simplex mass is 0.3034934467739529 versus approximately 0.291701 for that comparator. This is not a uniform size improvement or an established significant aggregate advantage.\\\",\n+    \\\"Explain the matched-center scaling comparison, especially IID Dirichlet K=8, alongside counterexamples: logistic-normal K=3 does not uniformly benefit from scaling, and shifted Dirichlet comparisons against simplex-coordinate regions reverse the aggregate utility ordering.\\\",\n+    \\\"Distinguish the ILR-box-volume objective used for training-only selection from the uniform-simplex probability mass measured in evaluation. Their different reference measures are factual; a causal account of any observed reversal is not established.\\\",\n+    \\\"Use the available paired seed bootstrap intervals, marginal Wilson intervals, feature-group records, and Monte Carlo resolution diagnostics at their actual levels. Retain unfavorable comparisons and intervals spanning zero; do not select only favorable nominal coverage levels.\\\",\n+    \\\"Do not report campaign winner margins, original-incumbent improvements, or scored/unscored attempt counts: downstream_study and study.* grounding entries are absent. Archived alternatives remain search-only context and cannot be mixed with final performance. The original initializer lacks matched final result records and is omitted as a performance comparator, not silently renamed as the unscaled ablation.\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"figure_plan\\\": [\n+    \\\"Figure 1, early conceptual overview: training-only scale selection, independent calibration, and two distinct evaluation axes; explicitly distinguish the ILR surrogate from simplex reference mass.\\\",\n+    \\\"Figure 2, coverage calibration: two dimension panels with all three response/covariate regimes, all nominal levels, uncertainty, and an identity reference.\\\",\n+    \\\"Figure 3, direct paired trade-offs: four panels showing utility and simplex-mass differences against the simplex-coordinate comparator, with dimensions separated and every nominal level retained.\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"figure_specs\\\": [\n+    {\n+      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"composition_reliability_overview\\\",\n+      \\\"required\\\": true,\n+      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Make the prediction target, training/calibration separation, scale-selection mechanism, and distinct reliability/efficiency estimands legible at a glance.\\\",\n+      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"diagram\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"generate-concept-figure\\\",\n+      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"scientific_stage_not_search_order\\\",\n+      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"observed-composition membership and uniform-simplex reference mass\\\",\n+      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"training-only fitting, independent calibration, evaluation\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n+      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 0.98,\n+      \\\"target_height_inches\\\": 1.6,\n+      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"The ICLR text column is the full 5.5-inch text width. A three-part horizontal overview at about 5.39 inches wide and 1.6 inches high allows short labels at 9–10 pt; do not shrink a crowded diagram. Place on page 1 if naturally possible, otherwise page 2, with about 0.30–0.35 page including caption.\\\",\n+      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n+      \\\"max_panels\\\": 3,\n+      \\\"max_panels_per_row\\\": 3,\n+      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 6,\n+      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 2,\n+      \\\"content\\\": \\\"Training X,Y -> fixed ILR center/base scale -> local-moment correction and nested pseudo-calibration selection; separate calibration Y -> finite-rank threshold -> region for an observed composition; evaluator -> coverage and uniform-simplex probe mass across IID/shift. Put all pseudo-calibration inside the training partition. Use no invented region shapes, numerical measurements, model architectures, or causal arrows from surrogate optimization to empirical gains.\\\",\n+      \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"],\n+      \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"results/ideas/03_training_only_conformal_rank_scale_selection/program_1/src/program.py\\\", \\\"task/evaluate_final.py\\\"],\n+      \\\"outputs\\\": \\\"Preserve editable draw.io source and prompt log. Prefer vector PDF exported from that source when available; otherwise regenerate the overview raster at least 2400 px wide. Never enlarge an existing raster. The later writing/orchestration stage owns generation.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"coverage_by_regime\\\",\n+      \\\"required\\\": true,\n+      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Expose near-target IID behavior and the shifted Dirichlet coverage deficit without hiding nominal-level dependence in an aggregate error.\\\",\n+      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"calibration_curve\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"matplotlib\\\",\n+      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"nominal_coverage\\\",\n+      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"mean observed-composition coverage\\\",\n+      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"K=3 and K=8\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n+      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 0.98,\n+      \\\"target_height_inches\\\": 2.45,\n+      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"Two panels side by side within the single 5.5-inch ICLR column give roughly 2.5-inch plotting widths. Three target values and three regime curves remain readable with shared axes, 9-pt ticks, a common legend, and a thin identity reference; budget about 0.40–0.45 page with caption. Use the observed data range, explicitly mark any truncated coverage axis, and do not force a zero origin that erases the IID differences.\\\",\n+      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n+      \\\"max_panels\\\": 2,\n+      \\\"max_panels_per_row\\\": 2,\n+      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 3,\n+      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 4,\n+      \\\"content\\\": \\\"For the frozen scaled-ILR method only, plot target coverage 0.80, 0.90, 0.95 against empirical coverage for logistic-normal IID, Dirichlet IID, and Dirichlet shift. The fourth series budget is reserved for the identity reference. This is a calibration comparison, not a temporal trajectory. Baseline comparisons appear in Figure 3 and the main table.\\\",\n+      \\\"uncertainty\\\": \\\"The writer may derive deterministic 95% seed-block percentile intervals from the eight existing per-seed coverage records, resampling seeds jointly across levels and cases. Such mean-coverage intervals are not already supplied in the stored uncertainty object and must be labeled as a new analysis of existing results. Do not average per-episode Wilson endpoints or treat test rows as eight-seed replicates.\\\",\n+      \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.metric.iid_coverage_error\\\", \\\"final.metric.shift_coverage_error\\\"],\n+      \\\"outputs\\\": \\\"Save reproducible plot source, vector figures/coverage_by_regime.pdf, and figures/coverage_by_regime.png at 300 DPI; LaTeX must reference the PDF.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"paired_region_tradeoffs\\\",\n+      \\\"required\\\": true,\n+      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Demonstrate condition-dependent utility and region-mass differences, including the shifted counterexamples to uniform dominance.\\\",\n+      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"paired_dot_whisker\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"matplotlib\\\",\n+      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"response_and_covariate_regime\\\",\n+      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"scaled ILR minus simplex-coordinate L-infinity: utility difference and uniform-simplex mass difference\\\",\n+      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"K and metric, arranged as a 2-by-2 grid\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n+      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 0.98,\n+      \\\"target_height_inches\\\": 4.2,\n+      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"Four compact forest panels need the full single-column text width, not a nominal half-column figure. Rows distinguish dimensions; columns distinguish metrics. Three short regime labels and three target markers fit at 9 pt. Use shared scales within each metric column, a common legend, and zero reference lines; budget about 0.60–0.70 page with caption. If labels collide, shorten labels and increase height within this budget rather than reducing type size.\\\",\n+      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n+      \\\"max_panels\\\": 4,\n+      \\\"max_panels_per_row\\\": 2,\n+      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 3,\n+      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 3,\n+      \\\"content\\\": \\\"Within each panel show logistic-normal IID, Dirichlet IID, and Dirichlet shift, with separate markers for alpha=0.05, 0.10, 0.20. Utility difference above zero favors scaled ILR; mass difference below zero indicates smaller regions. These signs have different meanings and must be stated separately, never collapsed into a single success rate. Show all levels rather than a favorable subset.\\\",\n+      \\\"uncertainty\\\": \\\"Use each case's paired_baselines.simplex_linf records: utility_difference with paired_seed_bootstrap95 and volume_difference with paired_volume_bootstrap95. Intervals are descriptive 95% seed-bootstrap intervals. Available bonferroni54_bootstrap_interval values apply to utility comparisons only and belong in the supplemental multiplicity table, not as volume uncertainty or exact familywise certificates.\\\",\n+      \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.metric.baseline_utility_gap\\\"],\n+      \\\"outputs\\\": \\\"Save reproducible plot source, vector figures/paired_region_tradeoffs.pdf, and figures/paired_region_tradeoffs.png at 300 DPI; LaTeX must reference the PDF.\\\"\n+    }\n+  ],\n+  \\\"supplementary_plan\\\": {\n+    \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Provide the reimplementation, complete paired comparisons, subgroup diagnostics, numerical-resolution limits, and audit trail needed to check the narrow main-paper thesis without hiding its shift failures or unfavorable baselines after the references.\\\",\n+    \\\"planned_subsections\\\": [\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Training-only fitting and selection details\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Give the Helmert basis, ridge design and penalties, nested three-fold residual construction, standardized local moments, Gaussian kernel bandwidths, shrinkage and clipping, swapped pseudo-calibration/evaluation halves, exact rank rule, log arithmetic mean ILR-box-volume objective, and Gaussian-selector/tiny-sample fallbacks. Link equations to the available selected source. Static fallback branches are not evidence of measured fallback frequency. Keep the main paper's core score and independence argument in the body.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"result.top_program_01\\\"],\n+        \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"results/ideas/03_training_only_conformal_rank_scale_selection/program_1/src/program.py\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Synthetic data and reproduction specification\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Document the frozen generator, feature map, logistic-normal noise, Dirichlet concentration function and positivity floor, K, independent train/calibration/test substreams, split sizes 1024/512/2048, all eight final seeds, three alpha values, and the uniform-simplex probe scheme. Preserve manifest hashes and documented dependencies. Record reproduction commands and script-to-figure mappings when the writer creates analysis scripts; distinguish documented budgets from measured runtime and do not invent hardware or environment versions.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.qualification.status\\\"],\n+        \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"task/evaluate_final.py\\\", \\\"task/resources/final/manifest.json\\\", \\\"task/requirements.txt\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Complete paired comparison and uncertainty tables\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Include every final case, alpha, and standard comparator with coverage, mass, utility, paired utility differences, paired mass differences, and their existing intervals. Include the supplied multiplicity-adjusted utility bootstrap intervals with their 54-comparison definition and small-seed limitation. The uncertainty object is repeated identically under each final episode: use it once, not as eight independent estimates. Include full matched-center unscaled-ILR and Bonferroni comparisons omitted from the compact main figure.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Feature-group reliability and information controls\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Report coverage for the evaluator's two x0-sign groups, their sample counts, and per-episode Wilson intervals; any across-seed group summary must be derived at seed level. Show constant-center, constant-input, and permuted-input controls, including stored paired feature-control utility differences. Describe the input transformations accurately and do not interpret them as training-time ablations or conditional-coverage guarantees. Treat this as a descriptive diagnostic, not a new subgroup-valid method.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"],\n+        \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"task/evaluate_final.py\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Reference-mass resolution and limiting controls\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Explain 128 sampled contexts and 1024 shared uniform-simplex probes per case, probe granularity, stored zero-hit counts and one-sided bounds, and the mean per-context Monte Carlo diagnostic. Shared probes correlate contexts: do not call that diagnostic a complete standard error for the final mean. A zero-hit estimate is not zero region mass. Include whole-simplex and zero-radius controls, and distinguish per-context resolution from the average across contexts.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"],\n+        \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"task/evaluate_final.py\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Evidence provenance and configuration scope\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Record final completion and qualification separately from performance, source/tree hashes, final versus search data separation, and the absence of a matched final original-initializer or selector-only comparison. Briefly document the joint-center/scale and Gaussian-selector alternatives as archived configurations with search-only evidence, not as ordered milestones or independent final replications. Do not invent study.* entries, campaign accounting, missing losses, search score deltas, or original-incumbent performance. Preserve any later qualification limitation in revision; unknown gate diagnostics stay unknown.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.qualification.status\\\", \\\"final.qualification.outcome\\\", \\\"final.qualification.correct\\\", \\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"result.top_program_02\\\", \\\"result.top_program_03\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"LLM Usage\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Give the precise roles documented in available local generation memories and source artifacts: research ideation, candidate implementation assistance, reference-query planning, and paper-framing assistance; add later writing and figure-generation roles only when they occur. Do not invent model versions, human oversight, independent checking, or undocumented tool use. Keep the disclosure anonymous.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"],\n+        \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"memory.md\\\", \\\"global_memory/memory.md\\\", \\\"results/ideas/03_training_only_conformal_rank_scale_selection/memory.md\\\"]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  },\n+  \\\"forbidden_headings\\\": [\n+    \\\"Search Progression\\\",\n+    \\\"Campaign Phases\\\",\n+    \\\"Milestones\\\",\n+    \\\"First Valid Result\\\",\n+    \\\"Winning Attempt\\\",\n+    \\\"Experiment A / Experiment B\\\",\n+    \\\"From Failure to Success\\\",\n+    \\\"Leaderboard\\\",\n+    \\\"Generalization Guaranteed\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"forbidden_phrases\\\": [\n+    \\\"correctness rate\\\",\n+    \\\"correct and eligible\\\",\n+    \\\"eligible\\\",\n+    \\\"combined_score\\\",\n+    \\\"rubric\\\",\n+    \\\"worker_01\\\",\n+    \\\"worker_02\\\",\n+    \\\"we finally found\\\",\n+    \\\"first successful attempt\\\",\n+    \\\"each iteration improved\\\",\n+    \\\"the search winner proves\\\",\n+    \\\"state-of-the-art performance\\\",\n+    \\\"uniformly smaller prediction regions\\\",\n+    \\\"guaranteed conditional coverage\\\",\n+    \\\"distribution-shift robustness is established\\\",\n+    \\\"zero probe hits prove zero volume\\\",\n+    \\\"rank selection causes the improvement\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"notes_for_writer\\\": [\n+    \\\"Use a thesis-first standalone structure: Introduction; Related Work; Prediction Regions and Evaluation Targets; Training-only Scale Adaptation; Experimental Design and Uncertainty; Reliability–Efficiency Results; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. These are scientific sections, not implementation or discovery chronology.\\\",\n+    \\\"Budget approximately 8.6 body pages, excluding references: Introduction/abstract/overview 1.2; Related Work 0.55; regions and estimands 0.9; method 1.35; design and uncertainty 1.05; results including Figures 2–3 and the summary table 2.35; discussion/limitations 0.95; conclusion 0.25. Figure space is already included. Reflow within 8–9 pages without changing template geometry; an eight-page body requires its last page at least 75% full. Spend spare space on equations, paired comparisons, uncertainty interpretation, and limitations, not extra chronology or decorative figures.\\\",\n+    \\\"The inspected supplied ICLR article template and paper.sty define one 5.5-inch text column and 9-inch text height. Full_width means that same text column, not a two-column float. A half-width inset would be about 2.64 inches at fraction 0.48 and is unsuitable for the required multi-panel figures. Keep 9-pt or larger final-size plot labels and captions consistent with venue style.\\\",\n+    \\\"Follow VENUE.md: anonymous ICLR 2026 submission, no names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, identifying links, or authorship-revealing first-person prior-work references. The supplied template contains a populated author placeholder and final-copy command; do not carry those into an anonymized submission unchanged. Preserve required infrastructure watermark behavior without introducing identifying authorship.\\\",\n+    \\\"Contributions are a fully specified training-only scale-adaptive configuration, a paired evaluation separating coverage and reference mass, and bounded empirical findings and failure diagnostics. Do not claim a new conformal theorem, comprehensive benchmark, unique new geometry, or causal selector advantage. The existing comparisons do not isolate rank selection from the Gaussian selector in the final plane.\\\",\n+    \\\"State the score and region explicitly: z=log(y)B, r=max_j |z_j-mu_j(x)|/s_j(x), q from order ceil((n_cal+1)(1-alpha)), and C(x)={positive simplex y:r<=q}. If the rank exceeds n_cal, the calibration threshold is infinite. Training pseudo-calibration has its own documented fallback. The standard split-conformal exchangeability argument is background, conditional on the fitted training-only rule; it is neither a measured guarantee under shift nor a conditional-on-X guarantee.\\\",\n+    \\\"Describe the correction as a covariate-dependent scalar multiplier shared across ILR coordinates, applied to positive diagonal base scales. The center and base-scale fits are fixed across the local-correction selectors; the evaluator's unscaled ablation instead sets all scales to one. Do not equate these two different controls.\\\",\n+    \\\"Define reference mass as probability under Dirichlet(1,...,1), averaged over sampled test contexts; it is normalized simplex volume, not ILR-box volume or an absolute Euclidean-volume number. Raw masses at K=3 and K=8 are not directly comparable as intrinsic efficiency rankings. Do not claim exact structural-zero support: responses are positive and the Dirichlet generator applies a declared positivity floor followed by closure.\\\",\n+    \\\"Define utility U=(1-V) max(0,1-|coverage-(1-alpha)|/(1-alpha)). Its absolute-error penalty includes overcoverage, and a higher U is not itself a coverage-validity certificate. Treat U as a benchmark-specific trade-off summary alongside the separate components, not as the paper's sole result or an externally standardized metric.\\\",\n+    \\\"Keep key quantitative anchors in the body, including the shifted failures and the selected method's larger aggregate mass than simplex-coordinate L-infinity. All new derived baseline aggregates, confidence intervals, rounding, and tables must be regenerated from the final records by saved analysis code; they are not additional experiments. Do not claim a significant overall utility advantage from the selected method's own score interval.\\\",\n+    \\\"Final status and qualification must be grounded only in final.qualification.status, final.qualification.outcome, and final.qualification.correct: this run reports completed, qualified, and true. Preserve that outcome without converting it to generalization, conditional validity, or superiority. If later authoritative evidence reports not_qualified, disclose it in writing, review, audit, and revision without attributing the cause unless gate evidence identifies it; missing diagnostics stay unknown.\\\",\n+    \\\"Use evaluator-owned method statistics, not unverified candidate diagnostics, as results. The evidence wrapper labels candidate_diagnostics_trust as unverified. Preserve final source/result hashes in the audit trail. The archived selected source is linked through the available configuration records and final evidence; do not claim an independently rehashed local final source tree if it has not been recovered and checked.\\\",\n+    \\\"Private-only grounding context: available identifiers use result.* and final.*, not study.*. Name the frozen evaluated method by its scientific mechanism. Omit campaign winner assertions, search-score margins, and scored/unscored accounting without the required study.* sources; never count missing measurements as losses. Search and final seed sets differ and must not be pooled into a larger replication count or compared as performance improvement over time.\\\",\n+    \\\"Use eight seeds as the outer repeated unit, not six cases times three alpha levels times eight seeds as independent experiments. Each seed wrapper repeats the same seed_block_uncertainty object; read one copy. For any derived aggregate comparison, resample complete seed blocks jointly across cases, alpha values, and methods. Stored Wilson intervals describe binomial test uncertainty within a fitted episode, not variability over retraining or simultaneous subgroup validity.\\\",\n+    \\\"Interpret existing paired bootstrap intervals descriptively with only eight seeds. The supplied 54-comparison adjusted utility endpoints are bootstrap percentile endpoints at adjusted tails, not a finite-sample familywise coverage theorem. Do not infer exact significance from them; no adjusted volume interval is supplied.\\\",\n+    \\\"The shift stress changes test covariates from training uniform support [-1.5,1.5] to clipped normal covariates on [-2.5,3]. It therefore includes support expansion and loss of exchangeability; it does not isolate which mechanism causes undercoverage. The conditional generator is retained, but support mismatch prevents promising that ordinary density-ratio weighting would repair the failures. No weighted-conformal comparator was evaluated.\\\",\n+    \\\"Keep the main limitations explicit: synthetic responses only, K=3 and K=8 only, fixed ILR basis and region family, pooled rather than subgroup calibration, no exact structural zeros, limited seeds and nominal levels, finite probe resolution, surrogate/reference-measure mismatch, absent final selector-only ablation, and no matched final original-initializer comparison. No CIFAR, classification-label coverage, latent-probability recovery, Hellinger comparison, Mondrian method, or real-data transfer result is supported.\\\",\n+    \\\"Use condition colors consistently across overview and quantitative panels; encode alpha by marker shape and method by labels or line style rather than reusing a condition color for a different meaning. Do not add trajectory plots: the nominal-level calibration axis is not a search iteration axis.\\\",\n+    \\\"Quantitative figure source must export both PDF and 300-DPI PNG. Reference PDFs from LaTeX whenever available. Raster-only full-width assets require at least 2400 pixels; any future column-width raster inset requires at least 1200 pixels. Regenerate from source rather than upscaling. Plan exactly one generate-concept-figure overview; do not run its rendering pipeline in this strategy stage.\\\",\n+    \\\"After references, use clearpage, appendix, and a Supplementary Information section with the grounded subsections above, including a separate LLM Usage section. Keep thesis-essential shift and unfavorable comparisons in the body. Later authoritative appendix rankings are additional routing instructions, not a reason to retroactively invent or rewrite this plan.\\\",\n+    \\\"Bibliography/provenance exist but contain many off-topic retrievals and bibliographic ambiguities. Use only relevant, actually consulted and verified sources; metadata is not a read-paper claim. Lei2016DistributionFreePI and Yang2021SelectionAA are potentially relevant existing keys, not automatically endorsed citations. Verify publication versions/years and scientific statements before use. Do not search for, identify, infer, access, or follow citation links to the prohibited study; stop and log any accidental encounter. Do not inflate related work with unrelated application citations.\\\",\n+    \\\"This stage creates strategy and memory artifacts only. Do not draft paper.tex or claims_grounding.json, generate figures, run new research experiments, or invoke final-paper validation here. Check strategy JSON syntax, required fields, source-ID existence, readability budgets, and memo agreement instead.\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"failure_reason\\\": \\\"\\\"\n+}\n*** Add File: paper_strategy.md\n+# Paper Strategy\n+\n+## Decision\n+\n+**Status:** ready for \"'`paper_standalone`, using `write-paper-standalone` for the later manuscript stage. This is a plan, not a paper draft.\n+\n+**Provisional title:** Scale-Adaptive Compositional Conformal Regions: Reliability and Limits\n+\n+**Central question:** Across the tested synthetic response distributions, dimensions, and covariate regimes, how do training-only scale-adaptive ILR conformal regions trade observed-composition coverage against uniform-simplex region mass relative to matched-center alternatives?\n+\n+**Central thesis:** In the six tested synthetic settings, scale-adaptive ILR regions exhibit condition-dependent reliability–efficiency trade-offs rather than uniform dominance: near-target IID coverage coexists with shifted Dirichlet undercoverage, and higher aggregate utility does not imply smaller simplex regions or conditional validity.\n+\n+The paper is one controlled empirical study of a fixed region family. Logistic-normal IID, Dirichlet IID, and shifted Dirichlet conditions, each at K=3 and K=8, are evidence blocks under the same question—not sequential experiments or a claim of transfer to arbitrary domains. The scientific value is a reproducible method specification and a reliability–efficiency assessment with concrete failures, not a claim that a small aggregate gain establishes a new universally superior method.\n+\n+## Framing Decision and Alternatives\n+\n+No edit context, seeded prior manuscript, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` bundle is present. The JSON decision `recentered` means narrowing the original broad task and proposed geometry audit to actual evaluated evidence; it does not assert that a prior paper was rewritten.\n+\n+| Candidate framing | Decision and grounded reason |\n+| --- | --- |\n+| Preserve an earlier broad geometry/boundary audit with local updates | Reject. No prior manuscript is available, and Hellinger/Euclidean geometry comparisons, structural-zero face audits, and Mondrian calibration were proposed rather than demonstrated. |\n+| Broaden into a cross-setting conformal method family | Retain only within the six synthetic conditions sharing the fixed ILR construction. No real-data, architecture, classification-label, or alternative-geometry transfer evidence supports a larger family claim. |\n+| Recenter on rank-selector superiority | Reject as the central thesis. Source code supports the mechanism, but the final evidence lacks a matched Gaussian-versus-rank selector ablation. Search-plane configurations do not establish a causal final-plane gain. |\n+| Recenter on reliability–efficiency trade-offs of the frozen scale-adaptive regions | Select. Final paired comparisons, independent calibration, multiple nominal levels, and uncertainty records support both favorable IID behavior and adverse shifted results. |\n+\n+Inspecting the selected source materially narrows the story: fixed Helmert ILR ridge centers, positive diagonal base scales, a bounded covariate-dependent scalar local-moment correction, and nested training-only rank/ILR-box-volume selection. The scalar correction is common to the ILR coordinates for a given covariate vector; it is not a separately learned full covariance geometry.\n+\n+## Final Evidence and Qualification\n+\n+Read `paper_data.json.final_evaluation` independently of any campaign summary. `downstream_study` and `study.*` grounding entries are absent. The final qualification sources explicitly report:\n+\n+- `final.qualification.status`: `completed`.\n+- `final.qualification.outcome`: `qualified`.\n+- `final.qualification.correct`: `true`.\n+\n+There is no failed qualification to disclose in this run. Qualification nevertheless does not establish superiority, conditional coverage, or generalization. If authoritative later artifacts report failed qualification, preserve that outcome and its limitations in writing, review, audit, and revision. Attribute a failure to statistical performance, validity, or adaptivity only when evaluator diagnostics identify the cause; missing gate diagnostics remain unknown.\n+\n+`final.evidence.episodes` contains eight final seed blocks, with six completed cases per seed and three alpha values per case. Final seeds are 1009, 1013, 1019, 1021, 1031, 1033, 1039, and 1049. Nominal coverage levels are 0.95, 0.90, and 0.80. This is not a set of independent case-by-alpha replications: seeds are the outer repeated unit. The identical uncertainty summary repeated under each seed wrapper must be read once.\n+\n+Important grounded anchors:\n+\n+| Quantity | Value and grounding |\n+| --- | --- |\n+| Studied method'\"'s mean IID absolute coverage error | 0.013562011718750005; \"'`final.metric.iid_coverage_error` |\n+| Studied method'\"'s mean shifted absolute coverage error | 0.13034261067708333; \"'`final.metric.shift_coverage_error` |\n+| Studied method'\"'s mean uniform-simplex mass | 0.3034934467739529; \"'`final.metric.mean_volume` |\n+| Studied method'\"'s mean utility | 0.661819668104677; \"'`final.metric.final_score` |\n+| Gap to the largest aggregate utility among standard evaluator comparators | 0.011594669497844157; `final.metric.baseline_utility_gap` |\n+| Simplex-coordinate L-infinity mean mass | Approximately 0.291701, derived from evaluator method records under `final.evidence.episodes` |\n+\n+These are retained measurements, not certification of a significant aggregate advantage. In particular, larger mean mass than the simplex-coordinate comparator contradicts a blanket size-improvement claim. Absolute coverage error is not signed undercoverage; signed coverage must be shown separately. The existing overall score interval cannot establish a confidence interval for the difference between methods.\n+\n+The wrapper labels candidate diagnostics as unverified. Use evaluator-owned membership and method statistics, not candidate assertions. Preserve evaluator/program/result hashes in the audit trail. The available selected source is linked by configuration records; do not assert that a locally recovered final source tree has been independently rehashed when it has not.\n+\n+## Method Groups and Comparison Roles\n+\n+1. **Training-only scale-adaptive ILR regions — studied/proposed configuration.** Explain the center, base scale, correction family, training-only selector, and independent pooled calibration. “Proposed” is an organizational role, not a claim of unique novelty.\n+2. **Unscaled ILR — matched-center ablation.** Set scales to one, retaining the same center and ILR basis. This compares the whole scaling package against no scaling; it does not isolate the correction or selector.\n+3. **Simplex-coordinate L-infinity — primary direct baseline.** Use the inverse-ILR center and a joint maximum-coordinate residual. Include all comparisons, not only conditions favoring the studied method. Its largest aggregate utility among standard comparators is an observed result, not a preregistered comparator-selection rule.\n+4. **Coordinatewise Bonferroni — simultaneous-coordinate baseline.** Use per-coordinate ranks at alpha/K. Include in the main summary table and complete supplemental comparisons.\n+5. **Feature-information and degenerate-region controls — supporting diagnostics.** Constant-center, constant-input, permuted-input, whole-simplex, and zero-radius records assess feature information and metric endpoints. They are not additional deployable method families or conditional-validity evidence.\n+\n+The original initializer source exists, but matched final performance records for it do not. Omit that performance comparison; do not relabel the unscaled ablation as the original initializer. Describe the archived joint-center/scale and Gaussian-selector configurations only in supplementary provenance as search-only alternatives, not final ablations. Without `study.*`, omit campaign winner claims, search-score margins, and scored/unscored accounting. Missing measurements must never become measured losses.\n+\n+## Scientific Organization and Results Views\n+\n+Use these body sections:\n+\n+1. Introduction.\n+2. Related Work.\n+3. Prediction Regions and Evaluation Targets.\n+4. Training-only Scale Adaptation.\n+5. Experimental Design and Uncertainty.\n+6. Reliability–Efficiency Results.\n+7. Discussion and Limitations.\n+8. Conclusion.\n+\n+The contribution list should be limited to a reimplementable training-only configuration, a matched-center evaluation separating reliability from region mass, and empirical condition-dependent findings with failure diagnostics. No new conformal theorem, comprehensive benchmark, or causal selector improvement is established.\n+\n+The main results should answer three scientific questions:\n+\n+- **Does measured coverage track the target?** Show all nominal levels, with IID and shifted conditions separate. Shift failure belongs in the body, not only the supplement.\n+- **When does scaling change the trade-off?** The IID Dirichlet K=8 comparison is informative, but retain the logistic-normal K=3 counterexample and shifted Dirichlet utility reversals. No uniform dominance claim survives those comparisons.\n+- **Does a higher utility mean smaller or more reliable regions?** No. Show separate coverage and mass alongside utility. Explain that the training selector minimizes an ILR-box surrogate, whereas evaluation uses uniform-simplex mass. Different measures are factual; their causal contribution to the observed results is unresolved.\n+\n+Include one compact main table with four method rows and columns for IID absolute coverage error, shifted absolute coverage error, mean reference mass, and utility. Recompute baseline aggregates from the final records using saved code. Equal weighting is across seeds, cases, and nominal levels, not a pooled mixture weighted by subgroup sample counts. Define utility rather than using an internal score label:\n+\n+`U = (1 - V) max(0, 1 - |coverage - (1-alpha)| / (1-alpha))`.\n+\n+Its error penalty includes overcoverage; it is a benchmark-specific summary, not a validity test or a standardized universal objective. Do not use a thresholded success-rate figure.\n+\n+## Required Figures and Layout\n+\n+The supplied ICLR template uses `article` and `paper.sty`. The inspected style sets one **5.5-inch text column** and **9-inch text height**. Here full-width and column-width refer to the same physical text width; there is no two-column `figure*` assumption. A 0.48-width inset would be approximately 2.64 inches and is unsuitable for these dense comparisons. The required figures use about 5.39 inches at fraction 0.98. Keep final-size labels at least 9 pt and shorten labels instead of shrinking a crowded figure.\n+\n+### Figure 1 — `composition_reliability_overview`\n+\n+- **Purpose:** immediate understanding of observed-composition prediction, training/calibration separation, and coverage versus reference mass.\n+- **Generator:** `generate-concept-figure`; exactly one overview uses this workflow. Do not render it in this stage.\n+- **Footprint:** full text width, approximately 5.39 by 1.6 inches; about 0.30–0.35 page including caption. Place on page 1 if natural, otherwise page 2.\n+- **Readability budget:** at most three panels in one row, six short nodes/categories per panel, and two information streams per panel; labels 9–10 pt.\n+- **Content:** training-only center/scale fitting and local-moment/rank-volume selection; separate finite-rank calibration; observed-composition membership and uniform-simplex probe evaluation across IID/shift. Pseudo-calibration stays inside the training partition. Label the ILR surrogate and simplex evaluation measure distinctly.\n+- **Grounding:** `final.evidence.episodes`; selected source at `results/ideas/03_training_only_conformal_rank_scale_selection/program_1/src/program.py`; evaluator definitions in `task/evaluate_final.py`.\n+- **Cautions:** no invented region measurements, alternative geometries, architectural blocks, or arrows implying a proven causal gain. This is a scientific data-partition schematic, not a research-run timeline.\n+\n+### Figure 2 — `coverage_by_regime`\n+\n+- **Purpose:** show near-target IID coverage and shifted Dirichlet deficits across every target level.\n+- **Generator:** matplotlib calibration curves, not a search progression plot.\n+- **Footprint:** full text width, approximately 5.39 by 2.45 inches; about 0.40–0.45 page with caption. Two panels side by side for K=3 and K=8.\n+- **Readability budget:** two panels; three target positions; three regime curves plus the identity reference, at most four series total per panel. Share axes and legend.\n+- **Content:** studied method only, target coverage 0.80/0.90/0.95 versus empirical coverage; logistic-normal IID, Dirichlet IID, and Dirichlet shift. Baseline evidence remains in Figure 3 and the main table. Use the observed coverage range and explicitly mark a truncated axis rather than flattening IID differences with an unhelpful zero origin.\n+- **Uncertainty:** the writer may derive deterministic 95% seed-block percentile intervals from the eight existing coverage records, resampling seeds jointly across cases and targets. These intervals are a later analysis of retained measurements, not an already available stored mean-coverage interval. Do not average per-episode Wilson endpoints.\n+- **Grounding:** `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.iid_coverage_error`, and `final.metric.shift_coverage_error`.\n+\n+### Figure 3 — `paired_region_tradeoffs`\n+\n+- **Purpose:** directly show condition-dependent utility and region-mass differences against simplex-coordinate L-infinity, including adverse shifted comparisons.\n+- **Generator:** matplotlib paired dot-and-whisker comparisons.\n+- **Footprint:** full text width, approximately 5.39 by 4.2 inches; about 0.60–0.70 page including caption. Four panels in a 2-by-2 grid; rows are K and columns are utility difference versus mass difference.\n+- **Readability budget:** four panels, two per row; three regime categories and three alpha markers per panel. Shared scales within each metric column; a common legend and zero-reference lines.\n+- **Content:** scaled ILR minus comparator, for every regime at alpha 0.05/0.10/0.20. Utility above zero favors scaled ILR; mass below zero indicates smaller regions. State both sign meanings explicitly rather than inventing one common success threshold.\n+- **Uncertainty:** use `utility_difference`/`paired_seed_bootstrap95` and `volume_difference`/`paired_volume_bootstrap95` under each case'\"'s \"'`paired_baselines.simplex_linf`. Descriptive 95% intervals are not simultaneous certificates. The stored adjusted utility endpoints are supplemental context and must not be applied to mass differences.\n+- **Grounding:** `final.evidence.episodes` and `final.metric.baseline_utility_gap`.\n+\n+Use consistent condition colors across the overview and plots; alpha can use marker shape and methods can use labels/line style. Never reuse a condition hue to mean a different method or threshold. Export quantitative figures from reproducible source as **PDF plus PNG at 300 DPI**, and reference the PDF from LaTeX. Preserve overview draw.io source and prompt logs; use a vector PDF export if available. Raster-only full-width figures require at least **2400 px** width, and any future column-width raster inset requires at least **1200 px**. Regenerate from source; never upscale an existing raster.\n+\n+## Body Budget and Venue Fit\n+\n+Target about **8.6 body pages**, excluding references, within `VENUE.md`'\"'s 8–9 page requirement:\n+\n+| Body material, including its figures/tables | Approximate pages |\n+| --- | ---: |\n+| Abstract, introduction, and early overview | 1.20 |\n+| Related work | 0.55 |\n+| Prediction regions and estimands | 0.90 |\n+| Training-only scale-adaptation method | 1.35 |\n+| Experimental design and uncertainty | 1.05 |\n+| Results, Figures 2–3, and summary table | 2.35 |\n+| Discussion and limitations | 0.95 |\n+| Conclusion | 0.25 |\n+\n+These allocations include figure space; do not add it again. If the body ends at eight pages, the final page must be at least 75% full. Reflow within nine pages without changing margins or fonts. Spend additional room on the split-independence argument, precise objectives, complete paired comparisons, uncertainty levels, and scope analysis—not filler, redundant plots, or chronological search details.\n+\n+Follow the anonymous ICLR 2026 brief: omit names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, and identifying links. The supplied template has a populated author placeholder and final-copy setting; do not preserve them unchanged in an anonymized submission. Preserve required infrastructure watermark behavior. Include a separate \"'`LLM Usage` section after the references.\n+\n+## Method and Statistical Guardrails\n+\n+- Define `z=log(y)B`, the maximum standardized ILR residual, independent split-calibration order `ceil((n_cal+1)(1-alpha))`, and the region membership rule. The evaluator uses an infinite threshold if the rank exceeds calibration size; the training pseudo-calibration selector has a distinct Gaussian fallback. State the usual exchangeability argument as background, not a new theorem or a conditional-on-X guarantee.\n+- Explain the actual scalar local correction, held-out selection, and fixed base fits. The unscaled ablation is not the unchanged-base-scale control, and no final local-correction-only or selector-only attribution is available.\n+- Measure `V` under Dirichlet(1,...,1), averaged over sampled contexts. It is normalized simplex volume, not ILR-box volume or an unnormalized Euclidean-volume measurement. Do not rank intrinsic efficiency by comparing raw K=3 and K=8 masses.\n+- Use eight seed blocks for variability over fitting and evaluation. Resample complete seed blocks jointly for any new aggregate contrast; do not pretend the repeated case/alpha cells are independent seeds. Saved Wilson intervals concern test uncertainty within a fitted episode and are not retraining or simultaneous subgroup intervals.\n+- Existing paired percentile intervals are descriptive with only eight seeds. The stored adjusted utility intervals use 54 comparisons and adjusted bootstrap tails; they are not an exact finite-sample familywise theorem. No corresponding adjusted mass interval is supplied.\n+- The reference-mass estimator uses 128 contexts and 1024 shared probes per case. Shared probes induce dependence across contexts. The stored mean per-context Monte Carlo diagnostic is not a complete standard error of the grand mean. Zero hits do not prove zero mass, and per-context probe resolution differs from averaged resolution.\n+- The shift recipe changes test X from uniform training support [-1.5,1.5] to a clipped normal on [-2.5,3], retaining the conditional generator. This includes support expansion as well as nonexchangeability. Undercoverage is measured; extrapolation, weighting, and model mechanisms are not separately identified. Do not promise that ordinary importance weighting would repair the result, and do not imply weighted conformal was evaluated.\n+- Keep limitations in the main paper: synthetic data only; fixed basis/region family; K=3 or 8 only; pooled calibration; no structural-zero evaluation; limited seeds/levels; Monte Carlo resolution; different selection/evaluation measures; missing final selector-only and original-initializer comparisons. Do not add CIFAR, label-set, latent-probability, Hellinger, Mondrian, or real-data claims.\n+\n+## Supplementary Information\n+\n+After references, use `clearpage`, appendix, and a **Supplementary Information** section. This adds substantive depth, not a dumping ground for adverse thesis-essential findings. Planned grounded subsections mirror the JSON:\n+\n+1. **Training-only fitting and selection details.** Basis, ridge design/penalties, three-fold residual construction, standardized moments, smoothing bandwidths, shrinkage/clipping, swapped pseudo-calibration halves, finite ranks, log arithmetic mean ILR-box objective, and static fallbacks. Ground in `final.evidence.episodes`, `result.top_program_01`, and the selected source. Do not invent realized fallback frequencies.\n+2. **Synthetic data and reproduction specification.** Generator equations, positivity floor, substreams, K, split sizes 1024/512/2048, final seeds/alphas, manifests, hashes, probe design, and documented NumPy dependency. Use `task/evaluate_final.py`, `task/resources/final/manifest.json`, and `task/requirements.txt`; grounding anchors `final.evidence.episodes` and `final.qualification.status`. The writer must save actual analysis commands and figure-script mappings, not invent runtime, hardware, or environment versions.\n+3. **Complete paired comparison and uncertainty tables.** Every case/alpha/standard baseline, full utility and mass contrasts, descriptive and adjusted utility endpoints, and the deduplicated uncertainty record. Ground in `final.evidence.episodes`. Keep essential shifted failures and unfavorable comparisons in the main body as well.\n+4. **Feature-group reliability and information controls.** Evaluator x0-sign groups, sample counts, Wilson intervals, and feature-control contrasts. Any mean group coverage interval must be computed at seed level. Ground in `final.evidence.episodes` and the evaluator. These are descriptive audits, not Mondrian validity or training-time feature ablations.\n+5. **Reference-mass resolution and limiting controls.** Shared probes, zero-hit bounds, per-context granularity, Monte Carlo diagnostic limitations, whole-simplex/zero-radius endpoints. Ground in `final.evidence.episodes` and evaluator definitions.\n+6. **Evidence provenance and configuration scope.** Separate qualification from performance and search from final evidence; retain source/result hashes and describe archived joint-center/scale and Gaussian-selector configurations without an ordered success story. Ground in `final.qualification.*`, `final.evidence.episodes`, `result.top_program_02`, and `result.top_program_03`. Do not report unsupported original-incumbent or campaign-count comparisons.\n+7. **LLM Usage.** Disclose documented ideation, implementation assistance, reference-query planning, and framing roles from local/global memories and source artifacts. Add later writing/figure-generation roles only when performed. `final.evidence.episodes` anchors the study; process disclosure is independently supported by `memory.md`, `global_memory/memory.md`, and the selected configuration'\"'s memory. Do not invent model versions, human oversight, or independent checks, and keep the disclosure anonymous.\n+\n+Later authoritative appendix-ranked analyses should join this supplement without retroactively inventing rankings in the strategy.\n+\n+## Citation and Language Discipline\n+\n+\"'`references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` now exist, but many retrieved entries are off-topic or have ambiguous publication versions. Metadata retrieval does not mean a work was read. `Lei2016DistributionFreePI` and `Yang2021SelectionAA` are potentially relevant existing keys, not automatically approved scientific support. Before citation, verify the relevant source, publication identity/year, and the claimed statement. Do not pad related work with unrelated applications or invent missing references.\n+\n+Do not identify, infer, search for, access, or follow citation networks to the prohibited study. No external literature browsing is part of this strategy stage. If later work accidentally encounters prohibited material, stop reading and record the location and amount seen.\n+\n+Forbidden headings include **Search Progression**, **Campaign Phases**, **Milestones**, **First Valid Result**, **Winning Attempt**, **Experiment A / Experiment B**, **From Failure to Success**, **Leaderboard**, and **Generalization Guaranteed**.\n+\n+Forbidden visible phrases include pipeline labels such as `correctness rate`, `correct and eligible`, `eligible`, `combined_score`, `rubric`, `worker_01`, and `worker_02`; chronology language such as “we finally found,” “first successful attempt,” and “each iteration improved”; and unsupported claims such as “state-of-the-art performance,” “uniformly smaller prediction regions,” “guaranteed conditional coverage,” “distribution-shift robustness is established,” “zero probe hits prove zero volume,” or “rank selection causes the improvement.” Internal source keys belong only in private grounding/audit context, not reader-facing result labels.\n+\n+## Handoff and Checks\n+\n+Implement the required figures before optional additions. Derive new summaries only from retained final records with saved analysis code; no new experiment is required for this strategy. This stage writes neither `paper.tex` nor `claims_grounding.json`, generates no figures, and does not run final-paper validation. Its checks are JSON syntax, required fields, source-ID existence, figure readability budgets, and agreement between this memo and the JSON.\n*** End Patch\nPATCH'","aggregated_output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA paper_strategy.json\nA paper_strategy.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
